# 2\. Strict low-poly environment asset specification

This is a critical section. The game must not accidentally become a mixture of extremely low-poly characters and highly detailed environment assets.

## 2.1 Geometry budget

All polygon budgets below refer to triangles, unless the existing software reports polygon counts differently.

| Asset category | Recommended triangle budget |
| --- | --- |
| Tiny debris | 4–12 |
| Small stones | 8–20 |
| Small crates | 12–30 |
| Barrels | 12–28 |
| Small signs | 4–12 |
| Sandbags | 8–20 |
| Small rocks | 8–24 |
| Large rocks | 16–40 |
| Small plants | 8–24 |
| Simple bushes | 12–35 |
| Small furniture | 12–35 |
| Streetlights | 12–35 |
| Small machinery | 20–50 |
| Military barriers | 12–30 |
| Vehicles | 50–120 |
| Large machinery | 60–150 |
| Watchtowers | 70–140 |
| Small buildings | 60–140 |
| Medium buildings | 100–220 |
| Large buildings | 150–300 |
| Main landmarks | 120–350 |

These are recommended starting targets, not a requirement that every asset must use every available triangle.

**Prefer fewer triangles whenever the silhouette remains readable.**

A simple crate should not use 100 triangles. A simple barrel should not use a detailed cylindrical mesh with dozens of sides.

## 2.2 Geometry construction rules

Use the following geometry methods:

- Cuboids for walls and boxes.
- Low-sided prisms for barriers.
- Angular cylinders for pipes and barrels.
- Triangular prisms for structural supports.
- Simplified irregular meshes for rocks.
- Small groups of angular shapes for foliage.
- Flat polygons for signs and panels.
- Low-sided cylinders for tanks.
- Simple tapered meshes for towers.
- Modular wall and floor segments for buildings.

Avoid:

- Subdivision surfaces.
- Dense bevel modifiers.
- Smooth high-resolution cylinders.
- Realistic sculpted surfaces.
- Excessive edge loops.
- Unnecessary internal geometry.
- Hidden faces.
- Excessively detailed damage meshes.
- High-poly imported asset packs.

## 2.3 Flat shading and materials

Use flat shading as the default.

Use a limited color palette for each map.

Each asset should normally use one or two materials.

For larger assets, three materials are acceptable when necessary for readability.

Use color variation to communicate form.

Example:

A concrete barrier:

- Main surface: medium grey.
- Upper surfaces: lighter grey.
- Side surfaces: darker grey.

A military crate:

- Main surface: military green.
- Metal reinforcement: dark grey.
- Small markings: muted yellow.

Do not use excessive textures to compensate for simple geometry.

## 2.4 Asset reuse

Create a common modular asset library.

Reuse the same underlying crate, barrel, wall, concrete barrier and industrial box meshes throughout the game.

Change their materials, rotations, scales and arrangements where appropriate.

Do not duplicate nearly identical meshes when one reusable model is sufficient.

Do not scale assets so extremely that their original silhouette becomes distorted.

## 2.5 Collision geometry

Visual geometry and collision geometry should be separate where practical.

Use:

- Boxes for buildings.
- Simple capsules or boxes for small props.
- Simplified convex shapes for vehicles.
- Simple floor and wall collision.
- Dedicated navigation geometry.

Do not use complex triangle-mesh collision for every small object.

Small decorative objects should not accidentally become major enemy navigation obstacles.

## 2.6 Low-poly visual quality

The result must look deliberately stylized, not unfinished.

Use:

- Strong silhouettes.
- Angular building outlines.
- Clear environmental color blocking.
- Distinctive landmarks.
- Restrained surface details.
- Carefully composed lighting.
- Large recognizable structural forms.

